 captured, reboot issued, post-restart boot_id 1b6ce12f... differs, and eth1 retained its 192.168.20.3/24 address with ping to node1 succeeding afterward; isolation from node2 re-confirmed post-restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3cc871edd4354007","cmd-89001de5d82b4813","cmd-136078c70b4b4628","cmd-ec4b5b343dd54e1d","cmd-2af51c252dc345ba"]}],"confidence":0.88,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Executor commands were largely discovery/verification or directly tied to building the VLAN segmentation. Notable state-changing actions: (1) on node1, removing docker0's class-E address and stopping interfaces-config.service (cmd-ec782803b9e04b7c) — a necessary prerequisite per the SONiC-VS reference to allow manual bridge configuration, later made persistent and re-applied automatically at boot via the new oneshot service, so not residue; (2) on node3, a temporary out-of-VLAN IP (192.168.10.3/24 then 192.168.10.99/24) was added twice purely to probe cross-VLAN isolation (cmd-4998a782a5894151, cmd-e9571d7223aa4e18, cmd-2af51c252dc345ba) and was removed immediately after each probe (cmd-e73ab4eb9cb74ef0 and inline in the same commands) — bounded, low-risk, fully reverted; (3) initial raw `ip addr` assignments on node2/node3 (cmd-167820c8046e427b, cmd-b9f1600446a44412) were silently reverted by NetworkManager and were then correctly superseded by persistent nmcli static profiles, not left as conflicting state. One new failed systemd unit, system-health.service, appears on node1 in the after_executor snapshot that was not present in before_prepare/after_prepare; watchdog-control.service was already failed pre-existing. The cause of the new system-health.service failure is not established in the trace (could be an artifact of the docker0/interfaces-config changes or unrelated SONiC-VS boot behavior) and is noted as a limitation rather than penalized further.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ec782803b9e04b7c","cmd-4998a782a5894151","cmd-e73ab4eb9cb74ef0","cmd-e9571d7223aa4e18","cmd-2af51c252dc345ba","cmd-167820c8046e427b","cmd-ca9b6dd2e4454d09","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct evidence: node1 was configured as a VLAN-aware bridge with eth1 (toward node2) as an untagged access port in VLAN 10 and eth2 (toward node3) as an untagged access port in VLAN 20; both node2 and node3 maintain working pings to node1's per-VLAN gateway; and cross-segment L2 isolation was directly demonstrated via arping/ping receiving zero responses even when node3 was given an address inside node2's subnet. Configuration was made persistent across reboot via a new systemd oneshot unit, and the provider-wide restart protocol was completed on all three nodes with pre-restart baselines, changed boot identities, and post-restart recovery of VLAN state, addressing, and connectivity/isolation, including a genuine transient race on node1's post-boot service ordering that was investigated and resolved rather than concealed.","limitations":["The cause of a new failed service (system-health.service) appearing on node1 only after the restart is not established by the captured evidence — it may be unrelated to the executor's changes (e.g. inherent SONiC-VS boot behavior) but this cannot be confirmed from the available commands.","Boot_id checks on all three restarts show several connection-reset/timeout probe attempts before success, which is expected during a live reboot but means the exact restart completion moment is inferred from the first successful post-reboot response rather than directly observed mid-transition.","node1's post-restart persistence check initially showed the VLAN configuration absent (service inactive, error output) due to a service-ordering race with interfaces-config.service; while this was followed up and shown resolved, it indicates the persistence mechanism's boot-time reliability is not fully hardened."]},"ttft